REFERENCE    :
Detailed description of the instrumentation and the method
of data reduction are given in the annual bulletin
"Observations Magnetiques" edited by the Bureau Central
de Magnetisme Terrestre (BCMT, Paris).

HISTORY      :
The Kourou magnetic observatory is located on the site
of the Guiana Space Center (CSG, also known as
Europe's Spaceport), near the Ariane launch complex.
It is run by IPGP in cooperation with the CSG, and is
partly funded by the Centre National d'Etudes Spatiales
(CNES). The equipments are installed and maintained by
IPGP. The GSC provides operational support for the
absolute measurements and observatory routines.

The first magnetic station in Kourou was installed in
1992, and continuous magnetic observations meeting the
INTERMAGNET requirements started in 1995. The Kourou
observatory was the first INTERMAGNET magnetic
observatory in South America. Following some instrument
failures, the equipments were fully upgraded in 1999.
A few more defects were fixed in 2000. The GEOMAG M390
vector magnetometer was replaced with a IPGP VM391 in
August 2007. 
The 29th of March 2012, the observatory was upgraded     
to one-second acquisition.

One-second values are processed by IPGP.

ABSOLUTE 
MEASUREMENTS :
Double absolute measurements are taken weekly.
Some measures missing in September as the theodolite 
needed maintenance (done in October 2021).

FAILURES     : 
All gaps are from deleted disturbances.

